    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.indentscope\n\n### Description\n\nRelated issue: https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/issues/445\n\nAssuming the following config:\n```lua\n      require('mini.indentscope').setup({\n        options = {\n          cursor = true, // default\n          try_as_border = true, // not important, but to just to make it concrete\n        },\n      })\n```\nLet me describe the pain point first. The `cursor = true` makes the cursor position important to decide the indent scope. When yanking an indent textobject `yai`, the cursor is placed on the first column afterwards. This makes the scope to change (it becomes the root indentscope). Doing further operation, for example, jumping to the bottom of the indent would result in a larger jump than the yanked range, which introduces friction.\n\nFor that reason, I think char-wise selection for indent textobject is more appropriate and has less friction, at least for `cursor = true`, since it preserves the indent scope before the yanking (or any operation). The behaviour in my mind is something like this:\n- `vai` will select the ranges from the first non-black character in the top indent to the last character in the bottom indent. \n- `Vai` will behave as the current implemented `vai`.\n\nAs an alternative, we can handle the cursor position for yanking so that it is positioned on the first non-blank character instead of the first column. But IMO, this feels a bit odd (not a strong opinion though).",
